(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-conduct"]={"plain":{"bills":{"conduct":{"slug":"conduct","title":"The Medical Care PAID Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-0-1","heading":"\u00a7 0-1. Short title","part":null,"body":"This Act may be cited as the \"Medical Care Protection Against Insurance Denials Act\" or the \"Medical Care PAID Act\"."},{"anchor":"sec-0-2","heading":"\u00a7 0-2. Findings","part":null,"body":"The Congress finds the following:\n\n**(1)** Health care in the United States is financed through many systems that operate side by side \u2014 traditional Medicare, Medicare Advantage, fifty-one Medicaid programs, Medicaid managed care, plans sold on the Affordable Care Act marketplaces, insured and self-insured employer coverage, the Federal Employees Health Benefits Program, the Veterans Health Administration and the Military Health System.\n\n**(2)** Those systems differ from one another in benefit design, payment method, utilization management and administration, and they serve overlapping populations in the same communities and often through the same clinicians.\n\n**(3)** Those differences constitute the largest body of comparative evidence about health care financing available in any country, and the United States does not systematically use it.\n\n**(4)** The principal obstacle is not analytic. The methods and data by which payers calculate performance against benchmarks are frequently not disclosed, and are treated by some payers as proprietary.\n\n**(5)** In *Gobeille v. Liberty Mutual Insurance Co.*, the Supreme Court held that the Employee Retirement Income Security Act of 1974 preempts a State law requiring health care payers to report claims data, as applied to plans governed by that Act, because the collection and maintenance of plan data is a core function of that Act. **States may not require self-insured group health plans to report.**\n\n**(6)** Self-insured group health plans cover a majority of Americans with employer coverage. A comparison of payers that omits them omits the largest single category.\n\n**(7)** The preemption described in finding 5 is a limit created by a statute of the United States. It does not limit the United States."},{"anchor":"sec-7-1","heading":"\u00a7 7-1. Network adequacy and directory accuracy","part":null,"body":"**(a) DIRECTORY ACCURACY.** \u2014 A health insurance issuer and a Medicare Advantage organization shall verify, at the interval set in the schedule, that each provider listed in its directory is accepting new patients covered by the plan, at the location and in the specialty listed.\n\n**(b) CONSEQUENCE ATTACHES TO THE RECORD.** \u2014 Where a provider is listed and is not accepting such patients, an enrollee who obtains care from that provider in reliance on the listing pays no more than the enrollee would have paid for an in-network provider, and the amount is credited toward the enrollee's deductible and out-of-pocket maximum.\n\n**(c) SELF-EXECUTING.** \u2014 Subsection (b) applies by reason of the listing and the enrollee's reliance, and requires no determination by the Secretary.\n\n**(d) PUBLICATION.** \u2014 The Secretary shall publish, by plan, the proportion of listed providers verified as accepting new patients. Publication is a ministerial duty."},{"anchor":"sec-7-2","heading":"\u00a7 7-2. Network composition for mental health and substance use disorder benefits","part":null,"body":"**(a) CODIFIED.** \u2014 A group health plan and a health insurance issuer offering coverage shall not impose a nonquantitative treatment limitation on mental health or substance use disorder benefits in any classification unless the plan's network composition, measured under subsection (b), does not produce a material difference in access as compared to medical and surgical benefits in the same classification.\n\n**(b) MEASURES.** \u2014 Network composition is measured by \u2014\n\n**(1)** the proportion of care obtained out of network;\n\n**(2)** time and distance to participating providers; and\n\n**(3)** the rates paid to participating providers, compared to a benchmark.\n\n**(c) THE STANDARD DOES NOT DEPEND ON ENFORCEMENT BEING ELECTED.** \u2014 Subsections (a) and (b) are effective on the date set in the schedule and are not conditioned on the issuance of any regulation, guidance, enforcement policy or safe harbor. **No suspension, non-enforcement policy or deferral of enforcement affects the obligation or its enforceability by any person authorized to enforce it.**"},{"anchor":"sec-7-3","heading":"\u00a7 7-3. Prior authorization","part":null,"body":"**(a) CLINICIAN REVIEW.** \u2014 A determination to deny, reduce or terminate coverage of a service on the ground that it is not medically necessary shall be made by a physician or other health professional **licensed in the same or a similar specialty** as the service under review.\n\n**(b) TIME LIMITS.** \u2014 A determination shall be made within the periods set in the schedule, which shall be shorter for a request identified as urgent.\n\n**(c) FAILURE TO DECIDE.** \u2014 Where a determination is not made within the applicable period, the service is authorized. **The consequence attaches to the passage of time and requires no further action by any person.**\n\n**(d) PUBLICATION.** \u2014 Each plan shall report, and the Secretary shall publish, the number of requests, the proportion denied, the proportion overturned on appeal, and the median time to determination, by service category.\n\n**(e) GOLD-CARDING.** \u2014 A plan shall exempt from prior authorization any provider whose requests in a service category were approved at or above the rate set in the schedule in the preceding period.\n\n**(e-1) THE EXEMPTION IS AUTOMATIC AND THE PLAN COMPUTES IT.** \u2014 A plan shall identify qualifying providers from its own records, apply the exemption without application, and notify the provider. **No provider may be required to apply for, track, document, or request an exemption.**\n\n**(e-3) PUBLISHED.** \u2014 Each plan shall report, and the Secretary shall publish, the number of providers exempted under this subsection by service category."},{"anchor":"sec-7-11","heading":"\u00a7 7-11. Medical debt and consumer reports","part":null,"body":"**(a) EXCLUSION.** \u2014 A consumer reporting agency may not include in any consumer report, and no person may furnish to a consumer reporting agency, information relating to a medical debt.\n\n**(b) NO USE.** \u2014 A creditor, and any person making a determination regarding credit, housing, insurance, or employment, may not use or obtain information relating to a medical debt for that purpose.\n\n**(c) MEDICAL DEBT.** \u2014 The term means a debt arising from the provision of a health care service, product, or device by a health care provider, **including a debt that has been assigned, sold, refinanced, or converted into another form of obligation, and including an extension of credit made or arranged for the purpose of paying for such a service, product, or device.**\n\n**(d) FORM DOES NOT CONTROL.** \u2014 Subsection (c) applies **regardless of the form, title, or characterization of the obligation**, and whether the creditor is the provider, an affiliate, a lender presented to the patient at the point of care, or an assignee. **A general-purpose credit account is within this section to the extent of amounts charged for a health care service, product, or device.**\n\n**(e) ENFORCEMENT.** \u2014 This section is enforced as though it were a requirement of the Fair Credit Reporting Act, and the remedies provided by sections 616 and 617 of that Act apply. **No new remedy is created and none is displaced.**\n\n**(f) NO CONTRARY AGREEMENT.** \u2014 A term of any agreement purporting to authorize furnishing, reporting, or use prohibited by this section is void."},{"anchor":"sec-7-14","heading":"\u00a7 7-14. Out-of-network payment \u2014 a benchmark replaces arbitration","part":null,"body":"**(b) THE BENCHMARK.** \u2014 Where a plan or issuer is required to cover an item or service furnished by a nonparticipating provider or facility, the total amount payable to the provider or facility is **the recognized amount**, which is the percentile set in the schedule of the contracted rates in effect for the same billing code, in the same geographic area, between plans and participating providers.\n\n**(c) ARBITRATION REPEALED \u2014 IN EVERY PLACE IT APPEARS.** \u2014 The independent dispute resolution process is repealed. This subsection reaches \u2014\n\n**(1)** section 2799A-1(c) of the Public Health Service Act;\n\n**(2)** section 2799A-2 of that Act, relating to air ambulance services;\n\n**(3)** section 716(c) of the Employee Retirement Income Security Act of 1974;\n\n**(4)** section 9816(c) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986; and\n\n**(5)** any other provision of law establishing, funding, or governing a process by which the amount payable for an item or service furnished by a nonparticipating provider or facility is determined by an entity selected by or for the parties.\n\n**No arbitration, mediation, or comparable proceeding determines the amount payable under subsection (b), and no party may initiate one.**\n\n**(c-1) THE REGULATIONS FALL WITH THE AUTHORITY.** \u2014 **On the effective date, every regulation issued under a provision repealed by subsection (c) ceases to have effect, and no such regulation may be relied on, applied, or enforced.**\n\n**(c-2) AIR AMBULANCE.** \u2014 Services furnished by an air ambulance are subject to the recognized amount under subsection (b) on the same terms as any other item or service, and the patient protections in subsection (i) apply to them.\n\n**(d) COMPUTATION AND PUBLICATION.** \u2014 The Secretary shall compute the recognized amount from the data reported under section 16-2, and shall publish each recognized amount, the number of contracted rates it rests on, and the method of computation. **Publication is a ministerial duty, and no plan, issuer, or provider computes the amount payable to itself.**\n\n**(e) THE FEEDBACK LOOP, CLOSED.** \u2014 In computing the recognized amount \u2014\n\n**(1)** only a rate established by an agreement between a plan or issuer and a participating provider may be counted; and\n\n**(2)** **an amount paid under this section, and any rate set by reference to a recognized amount, may not be counted.**\n\n**(f) DIVERGENCE PUBLISHED, AND IT TRIGGERS A LOOK.** \u2014 The Secretary shall publish annually, for each geographic area, the recognized amount alongside the median contracted rate from which it is drawn. **Where the two diverge by more than the band set in the schedule, the Secretary shall publish an analysis of whether the recognized amount is altering the rate at which providers contract**, and shall transmit it to the Congress.\n\n**(g) THIN MARKETS.** \u2014 Where the number of contracted rates available for a billing code in a geographic area is below the number set in the schedule, the Secretary shall widen the geographic area until that number is met. Where it cannot be met, the recognized amount is the multiple of the amount payable under title XVIII set in the schedule. **The multiple shall be set so that it approximates the recognized amount in the nearest area in which the threshold is met**.\n\n**(h) NO INTENSITY EXCEPTION.** \u2014 No adjustment to the recognized amount is available on the ground that a particular case was more complex, more severe, or more resource-intensive than others billed under the same code.\n\n**(i) THE PATIENT'S POSITION IS UNCHANGED.** \u2014 The individual owes no more than the cost sharing that would apply to a participating provider, that amount counts toward the deductible and out-of-pocket maximum, **and no provider or facility may bill the individual for any difference between its charge and the recognized amount.**\n\n**(j) NO CONTRARY AGREEMENT.** \u2014 A term purporting to permit payment or billing otherwise than as provided in this section is void."},{"anchor":"sec-8-1","heading":"\u00a7 8-1. Repeals","part":null,"body":"**(a) SHORT-TERM LIMITED-DURATION INSURANCE.** \u2014 Coverage that is medically underwritten, that excludes coverage of a pre-existing condition, or that is not required to cover essential health benefits may not be offered as, or in a manner suggesting it is, individual health insurance coverage.\n\n**(b) TOBACCO SURCHARGE REPEALED.** \u2014 Tobacco use is repealed as a permitted rating factor for health insurance coverage.\n\n**(c) CONDITIONS ON ELIGIBILITY.** \u2014 A requirement that an individual document employment, work activity or hours as a condition of eligibility for medical assistance is repealed.\n\n**(d) RETROACTIVE COVERAGE.** \u2014 The restriction on retroactive eligibility for medical assistance is repealed.\n\n**(e) SIX-MONTH REDETERMINATIONS.** \u2014 A requirement that eligibility for medical assistance be redetermined more frequently than once every 12 months is repealed.\n\n**(f) COST SHARING FOR EXPANSION ADULTS.** \u2014 Authority to impose cost sharing on an individual eligible for medical assistance under section 1902(a)(10)(A)(i)(VIII) of the Social Security Act, above the amount permitted for any other individual eligible under section 1902(a)(10)(A)(i), is repealed.\n\n**(g) IMMIGRANT ELIGIBILITY RESTRICTIONS.** \u2014 The restrictions on eligibility for medical assistance, and for coverage through an Exchange, based on immigration status, other than the requirement of lawful presence, are repealed. **An individual lawfully present in the United States is eligible on the same terms as any other individual**, and no waiting period applies by reason of immigration status.\n\n**(h) PROVIDER TAX RESTRICTION RETAINED.** \u2014 The restriction on provider taxes is not repealed."},{"anchor":"sec-9-2","heading":"\u00a7 9-2. Related-party transactions, the medical loss ratio, and the retention cap","part":null,"body":"**(a) INTERCOMPANY TRANSACTIONS AT COST.** \u2014 In computing any ratio of incurred claims to premium revenue, an amount paid by an issuer to a person under common ownership or control with the issuer is recognized **at the cost incurred by that person in furnishing the item or service**, and not at the amount paid.\n\n**(b) RETENTION CAP.** \u2014 The amount an issuer may retain for administration and profit shall not exceed the amount per member per month set under subsection (c).\n\n**(c) HOW THE AMOUNT IS SET.** \u2014 The amount is a stated percentile of observed risk-adjusted administration-plus-profit per member per month within the market segment, computed from reported data and recalculated on the cycle set in the schedule. **It is segment-specific.**\n\n**(c-1) THE REPORTING THIS SECTION RUNS ON IS REQUIRED HERE, NOT BORROWED.** \u2014 Each issuer shall report annually, and the Secretary shall publish, **the amount of premium revenue, the amount paid for care, and the amount retained for administration and for profit, stated separately and per member per month**, by market segment. **This is the data from which the amount under subsection (c) is computed.**\n\n**(d) THE CAP IS ONE-WAY.** \u2014 On each recalculation, if the percentile has fallen, the cap falls with it. **If the percentile has risen, the cap rises only by the index set in the schedule.**\n\n**(e) EXCESS RETENTION IS REBATED, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES THE CAP A CAP.** \u2014 Where an issuer's retention for a period exceeds the amount permitted under subsection (b), **the excess shall be returned to the enrollees from whom it was collected** \u2014 as a credit against premium where coverage continues, and otherwise as a payment \u2014 in proportion to the months of coverage. The obligation arises on the computation and requires no finding, application, or proceeding.\n\n**(e-2) RELATION TO THE MINIMUM-RATIO REQUIREMENT \u2014 THE OLDER RULE STAYS.** \u2014 Where a rebate is due under subsection (e) for a period, **no rebate is owed for that period under the minimum-ratio requirement**, and the greater of the two amounts is payable. **The minimum-ratio requirement otherwise remains in effect.**\n\n**(e-3) NO WAIVER.** \u2014 The obligation under subsection (e) may not be waived, settled for less, or offset against any other amount.\n\n**(f) INDEX.** \u2014 The index under subsection (d) shall be a measure of general wage or price growth. **It shall not be a measure of medical prices.**"},{"anchor":"sec-9-3","heading":"\u00a7 9-3. Premium steering","part":null,"body":"**(a) PROHIBITION.** \u2014 A provider of health care services, and a person affiliated with or funded by such a provider, may not pay, or arrange for the payment of, an individual's premium for coverage where the payment has the effect of changing the source or amount of payment the provider receives for services furnished to that individual.\n\n**(b) DISCLOSURE.** \u2014 Any payment of an individual's premium by a third party shall be disclosed to the issuer and to the individual, identifying the payer and any provider affiliated with the payer.\n\n**(c) NOT A BAR ON CHARITY.** \u2014 This section does not prohibit payment of a premium by a person who is not a provider of the services concerned, is not affiliated with such a provider, and is not funded by such a provider."},{"anchor":"sec-9-5","heading":"\u00a7 9-5. Cost-sharing assistance counts","part":null,"body":"**(a) RULE.** \u2014 Any amount paid by or on behalf of an individual toward a covered item or service, including an amount paid under a manufacturer's assistance program, counts toward the individual's deductible and out-of-pocket maximum."},{"anchor":"sec-9-7","heading":"\u00a7 9-7. Compensation tied to the price of what is selected \u2014 one rule, two industries","part":null,"body":"**(a) GROUP PURCHASING ORGANIZATIONS.** \u2014 Compensation paid to a group purchasing organization by a vendor may not be determined by reference to the price or value of the items purchased. **Compensation shall be a fixed fee for services rendered.**\n\n**(b) PHARMACY BENEFIT MANAGERS.** \u2014 Compensation paid to a pharmacy benefit manager, and any amount it retains, may not be determined by reference to the list price of a drug, the size of a rebate, or any discount or price concession. **Compensation shall be a fixed fee for services rendered.**\n\n**(c) PASS-THROUGH.** \u2014 A rebate, discount or other price concession obtained by a pharmacy benefit manager shall be passed through in full to the plan or, where applicable, to the individual at the point of sale.\n\n**(d) SPREAD PRICING.** \u2014 A pharmacy benefit manager may not retain the difference between the amount charged to a plan and the amount paid to a pharmacy.\n\n**(e) ONE PATTERN, STATED ONCE.** \u2014 Subsections (a) and (b) state a single rule: **a person selecting what is purchased may not be paid more for selecting a more expensive thing.** The safe harbor permitting group purchasing compensation as a percentage of the value of purchases is repealed.\n\n**(f) DISCLOSURE.** \u2014 Compensation and retained amounts under this section shall be reported to the Secretary and published in aggregate."},{"anchor":"sec-16-1","heading":"\u00a7 16-1. Definitions","part":null,"body":"**(a) COVERED PAYER.** \u2014 The term \"covered payer\" means \u2014\n\n**(1)** the Secretary, with respect to benefits under parts A, B and D of title XVIII;\n\n**(2)** a Medicare Advantage organization;\n\n**(3)** a State, with respect to its plan under title XIX or its child health plan under title XXI;\n\n**(4)** a Medicaid managed care organization, prepaid inpatient health plan or prepaid ambulatory health plan;\n\n**(5)** a health insurance issuer offering coverage in the individual, small group or large group market;\n\n**(6)** a group health plan, including a self-insured group health plan;\n\n**(7)** a carrier under chapter 89 of title 5;\n\n**(8)** the Secretary of Veterans Affairs, with respect to the Veterans Health Administration; and\n\n**(9)** the Secretary of Defense, with respect to the Military Health System.\n\n**(b) ANTI-SUBSTITUTION.** \u2014 An entity is a covered payer if it performs substantially the function described in subsection (a) \u2014 bearing or administering the risk or cost of health care for a defined population \u2014 **regardless of the form, title or characterization of the entity or the arrangement, and regardless of the authority under which it operates.** The enumeration in subsection (a) is illustrative and not limiting.\n\n**(c) COMPARATIVE PERFORMANCE MEASURE.** \u2014 The term \"comparative performance measure\" means a measure of what happens to patients, computed identically across covered payers."},{"anchor":"sec-16-2","heading":"\u00a7 16-2. Reporting","part":null,"body":"**(a) DUTY.** \u2014 Each covered payer shall report to the Secretary, in the standardized format prescribed under subsection (c) \u2014\n\n**(1)** enrollment and demographic information sufficient to characterize the population served;\n\n**(2)** claims or encounter records for items and services furnished; and\n\n**(3)** the amounts paid, and the amounts for which the enrollee was responsible.\n\n**(b) THIS IS A FEDERAL DUTY AND IS NOT AFFECTED BY SECTION 514 OF ERISA.** \u2014 The duty in subsection (a) is imposed by this Act on covered payers directly. Nothing in section 514 of the Employee Retirement Income Security Act of 1974 limits it, and no covered payer is excused from it by reason of being a self-insured group health plan.\n\n**(c) FORMAT.** \u2014 The Secretary shall prescribe a single standardized reporting format, and shall use the format developed under section 115 of division BB of the Consolidated Appropriations Act, 2021 as its starting point rather than developing a new one.\n\n**(d) COMPLETENESS OF ENCOUNTER DATA.** \u2014 A covered payer described in section 16-1(a)(2) or (a)(4) shall report encounter records for every item and service furnished to an enrollee, whether or not payment was made on a fee-for-service basis. **A record is not complete if it omits items or services on the ground that the payer's payment to the provider did not vary with them.**\n\n**(e) MEANS FURNISHED.** \u2014 The Secretary shall furnish, at no charge, the interface by which reports are made, and shall provide for reporting by a covered payer that lacks the electronic means to use it.\n\n**(f) SMALL PLAN THRESHOLD.** \u2014 Subsection (a) does not apply to a group health plan with fewer enrollees than the number set in the schedule."},{"anchor":"sec-16-3","heading":"\u00a7 16-3. No proprietary claim defeats the duty","part":null,"body":"**(a) RULE.** \u2014 No covered payer may withhold information required by section 16-2, and no such information is exempt from publication under section 16-5, on the ground that it is a trade secret, is commercially sensitive, is confidential, or is proprietary.\n\n**(b) CONTRACT.** \u2014 A provision of any contract purporting to prohibit or condition reporting required by this Act is void.\n\n**(c) LIMIT.** \u2014 This section does not authorize the disclosure of individually identifiable health information except as section 16-6 permits."},{"anchor":"sec-16-4","heading":"\u00a7 16-4. Comparability","part":null,"body":"**(a) RISK ADJUSTMENT.** \u2014 The Secretary shall establish and publish a method for adjusting comparative performance measures for differences in the populations served by covered payers.\n\n**(b) THE METHOD IS PUBLISHED, NOT MERELY APPLIED.** \u2014 The Secretary shall publish the method, the data used to construct it, and the effect of the adjustment on each reported measure.\n\n**(c) EXISTING WORK.** \u2014 In establishing the method the Secretary shall consider the risk-adjustment methods developed for all-payer comparative performance reporting by States operating all-payer claims databases.\n\n**(c-1) THE UNADJUSTED MEASURES ARE PUBLISHED TOO.** \u2014 The Secretary shall publish, alongside each adjusted measure, **the unadjusted measure and the data necessary to reconstruct the adjustment.** **A person may compute a comparison from the unadjusted measures, and this section does not make the Secretary's adjustment the only permitted comparison.**\n\n**(d) LIMITS STATED.** \u2014 The Secretary shall publish, with each comparative performance measure, the respects in which the populations compared differ in ways the adjustment does not capture."},{"anchor":"sec-16-5","heading":"\u00a7 16-5. Publication","part":null,"body":"**(a) DUTY.** \u2014 The Secretary shall publish, not less often than annually, comparative performance measures by covered payer and by category of covered payer.\n\n**(b) MINISTERIAL.** \u2014 Publication under this section is a ministerial duty and is enforceable by any person by an action in the nature of mandamus.\n\n**(c) DATA AVAILABLE FOR RESEARCH.** \u2014 The Secretary shall make reported data available for research on comparative payer performance, in de-identified or limited-data-set form, on terms that do not permit a covered payer to approve, condition or delay a study.\n\n**(d) NO PAYER APPROVAL.** \u2014 No covered payer has a right to review, comment on, approve or delay a publication under this section before it is made."},{"anchor":"sec-16-6","heading":"\u00a7 16-6. Privacy","part":null,"body":"**(a) IDENTIFIABLE INFORMATION.** \u2014 Individually identifiable health information reported under this Act may be used and disclosed only for the purposes of this Act, and only in the minimum necessary form.\n\n**(b) PUBLICATION IS DE-IDENTIFIED.** \u2014 Publication under section 16-5 shall be in a form that does not identify any individual, determined under the expert determination method.\n\n**(c) PERMITTED USES ARE CLOSED.** \u2014 Information reported under this Act may not be used or disclosed \u2014\n\n**(1)** for any coverage, eligibility, rating, underwriting or payment determination as to any individual;\n\n**(2)** for utilization review adverse to a patient;\n\n**(3)** for any law enforcement or immigration purpose; or\n\n**(4)** for sale, licensing or transfer for value.\n\n**(c-1) CLOSURE.** \u2014 The uses and disclosures permitted by this Act are those and only those stated in it. No other authority supplies a further permitted use unless it amends this section by express reference, and the omission of any use is a determination of Congress and not a gap."},{"anchor":"sec-16-7","heading":"\u00a7 16-7. State all-payer claims databases \u2014 the *Gobeille* bar is lifted","part":null,"body":"**(a) SAVINGS CLAUSE.** \u2014 Section 514 of the Employee Retirement Income Security Act of 1974 does not preempt a State law requiring a group health plan, or an entity acting on its behalf, to report health care claims or encounter data to a database maintained by or for that State.\n\n**(b) FORMAT.** \u2014 A State law described in subsection (a) shall accept the standardized format prescribed under section 16-2(c), and a plan reporting in that format satisfies the State requirement.\n\n**(c) ONE REPORT.** \u2014 A covered payer that reports under section 16-2 shall not be required by a State to report the same data element separately, and the Secretary shall make reported data available to the State."},{"anchor":"sec-16-8","heading":"\u00a7 16-8. Enforcement","part":null,"body":"**(a) CIVIL MONETARY PENALTY.** \u2014 A covered payer failing to report as required is subject to a civil monetary penalty, scaled to the number of enrollees, in the amount set in the schedule.\n\n**(b) THE VIOLATION IS THE FAILURE TO REPORT.** \u2014 No penalty attaches under this Act by reason of the content of a report, or by reason of a covered payer's comparative performance.\n\n**(c) PUBLICATION OF COMPLIANCE.** \u2014 The Secretary shall publish the reporting compliance of each covered payer. Publication under this subsection is a ministerial duty."},{"anchor":"sec-16-9","heading":"\u00a7 16-9. Convergence \u2014 amendments to section 1115A of the Social Security Act","part":null,"body":"**(a) REACH.** \u2014 The authority to expand under section 1115A(c) extends to a model applicable to \u2014\n\n**(1)** a group health plan, including a self-insured group health plan;\n\n**(2)** a health insurance issuer;\n\n**(3)** the Veterans Health Administration; and\n\n**(4)** the Military Health System.\n\n**(b) EVIDENCE FROM OBSERVED PRACTICE, NOT ONLY FROM TESTED MODELS.** \u2014 The Secretary may exercise the authority under section 1115A(c) on the basis of an evaluation of a payment or delivery approach **already in use by one or more covered payers**, whether or not that approach has been tested as a model under section 1115A(b).\n\n**(b-1) STANDARD FOR SUCH AN EVALUATION.** \u2014 An evaluation under subsection (b) shall be conducted using data reported under section 16-2, adjusted under section 16-4, and shall be published with its method before the authority is exercised.\n\n**(c) THE EXPANSION TEST.** \u2014 Section 1115A(c) is amended so that the Secretary may expand where expansion is expected to \u2014\n\n**(1)** reduce spending without reducing the quality of care;\n\n**(2)** improve the quality of care without increasing spending; or\n\n**(3)** **materially improve the quality of care, or health outcomes, at an increase in spending that the Secretary determines is warranted by the improvement.**\n\n**(c-1) THE ACTUARY CERTIFIES THE AMOUNT; THE ACTUARY DOES NOT DECIDE WHETHER IT IS WORTH IT.** \u2014 Where the Secretary proceeds under subsection (c)(3), the Chief Actuary shall certify the expected effect on net program spending, stated as an amount. **The certification is of the magnitude and not of the merit, and the absence of a certification that spending would not increase does not bar expansion under subsection (c)(3).**\n\n**(c-2) BOUNDED.** \u2014 An expansion under subsection (c)(3) requires the Secretary to publish, before the rule takes effect, the improvement expected, the measure by which it will be assessed, the certified spending effect, and the basis for the determination that the increase is warranted. **The determination is reviewable for whether those things were published and considered, and not for the weight the Secretary gave them.**\n\n**(d) BENEFITS PRESERVED.** \u2014 Section 1115A(c)'s requirement that expansion not deny or limit the coverage or provision of benefits is unaffected and applies to every expansion under this section.\n\n**(e) NO DIMENSION-WIDE MANDATE BY IMPLICATION.** \u2014 Nothing in this section authorizes the Secretary to require that covered payers be identical in any respect other than the specific approach expanded under section 1115A(c)."},{"anchor":"sec-16-10","heading":"\u00a7 16-10. Rule of construction \u2014 what this Act does not do","part":null,"body":"**(a)** Nothing in this Act requires any covered payer to adopt the benefit design, provider network, payment method or administrative practice of any other covered payer, except as expanded under section 16-9.\n\n**(b)** Nothing in this Act consolidates any covered payer into another, or creates a single system of health care financing.\n\n**(c)** Nothing in this Act limits a covered payer from adopting an approach more favorable to enrollees than one expanded under section 16-9."},{"anchor":"sec-27-1","heading":"\u00a7 27-1. Definitions, and the trigger that cannot be papered over","part":null,"body":"**(a) OFFERING EMPLOYER.** \u2014 A business is an **offering employer** if it pays for or subsidizes health coverage for **any individual** who performs services for it \u2014 **regardless of the form, title, or characterization of the individual (employee, partner, owner, director, or contractor), of the arrangement (a group health plan, a reimbursement arrangement, an individual-coverage account, or any other), or of the coverage.**\n\n**(b) CHAIN WORKERS.** \u2014 Workers in the business's chain, per the definitions of the Real Employer Act, are within this title as \u00a7 27-3 provides.\n\n**(c) LOOKBACK.** \u2014 A business that was an offering employer at any time in the 12 months before the **date of introduction of this Act** is an offering employer for purposes of \u00a7 27-4, whatever it later does."},{"anchor":"sec-27-2","heading":"\u00a7 27-2. Same plan, same percentage","part":null,"body":"**(a)** An offering employer shall offer to every employee **the same coverage, at the same employer contribution percentage**, prorated continuously by full-time-equivalent fraction \u2014 a 0.5 FTE is offered the same plan at half the employer subsidy, **and no hours threshold, waiting tier, or classification may vary either.**\n\n**(b)** A collectively bargained plan is deemed compliant for the workers it covers.\n\n**(c)** No provision of this title requires any employer to offer coverage \u2014 the obligation attaches only to offering, and only equality is required of it."},{"anchor":"sec-27-3","heading":"\u00a7 27-3. The chain gets the percentage","part":null,"body":"For a chain worker not on the employer's plan, the offering employer owes **contribution parity** \u2014 payment, at its \u00a7 27-2 percentage applied to the benchmark premium, toward the worker's own coverage or into the program on the worker's behalf \u2014 never plan enrollment across employer lines."},{"anchor":"sec-27-4","heading":"\u00a7 27-4. The employee's money follows the employee, and the ratchet holds through transition","part":null,"body":"**(a) CONTRIBUTION-FOLLOWS.** \u2014 Where an individual offered coverage elects the program instead, the offering employer pays its \u00a7 27-2 percentage, **applied to the benchmark premium**, to the program on that individual's behalf.\n\n**(b) THE HIGH-WATER RATCHET, FOR THE TRANSITION PERIOD.** \u2014 Through the period set in the schedule, an offering employer's contribution percentage may not fall below its highest level since the lookback date, **and the obligation under this section survives the termination of the employer's plan** \u2014 dropping coverage changes where the money goes, never whether it is owed.\n\n**(c)** Aggregation under IRC \u00a7 414(b)/(c) and predecessor-successor rules apply; a reorganized or successor business inherits the lookback status and the high-water mark.\n\n**(d)** Money paid under this section is the individual's premium payment for all purposes, is never cash to the individual, and is excluded from the individual's income **the \u00a7 17-5(d-1) class**."},{"anchor":"sec-27-5","heading":"\u00a7 27-5. The purchase lane","part":null,"body":"An employer of any size \u2014 **no minimum** \u2014 may enroll its workforce in the program at the benchmark premium, paying its \u00a7 27-2 percentage (or, for a non-offering employer electing the lane, the percentage it states, applied uniformly), with enrollment requiring **no more information than identifies the employer, the workers, and the percentage**."},{"anchor":"sec-27-6","heading":"\u00a7 27-6. Enforcement and closure","part":null,"body":"**(a)** The protections of this title are enforceable by the worker by civil action, with \u00a7 46-6's fee and forum rules applying; the \u00a7 62(e) conforming applies.\n\n**(b)** Pay-or-play for never-offering employers, and this title's permanent successor after the transition ratchet, **are reserved expressly to the funding stage** \u2014 their omission here is a determination of Congress and a scheduled decision, not a gap.\n\n**(c)** No agency may exempt, tier, or condition anything in this title by regulation; the equality rules state their own content."},{"anchor":"sec-28-1","heading":"\u00a7 28-1. Protected acts","part":null,"body":"No person shall discharge, discipline, deactivate, terminate or decline to renew the contract of, reduce the privileges, compensation, hours, or patient assignments of, or otherwise take or threaten an action adverse to a clinician because the clinician \u2014\n\n**(1)** reported, or prepared to report, a concern of patient safety, quality, staffing, boarding, or facility condition, internally or to any authority;\n\n**(2)** advocated for the care of a patient, including against a coverage, discharge, or utilization decision;\n\n**(3)** furnished information, or is identifiable, in any proceeding under this Act or its companion Acts; or\n\n**(4)** declined to commit an act the clinician reasonably believed unlawful or below the standard of care.\n\nAn action is adverse if it would tend to deter a reasonable clinician from any act stated. **This section reaches every clinician \u2014 employed, contracted, privileged, or engaged through any intermediary or chain, regardless of the form, title, or characterization of the arrangement.**"},{"anchor":"sec-28-2","heading":"\u00a7 28-2. Causation and defenses \u2014 and these are the defenses","part":null,"body":"A violation is established where a protected act was a **contributing factor**; the only defense is clear and convincing evidence that the same action would have been taken absent every protected act. The common law supplies no defense, and the omission of a defense is a determination of Congress and not a gap."},{"anchor":"sec-28-3","heading":"\u00a7 28-3. Due process before termination or deactivation","part":null,"body":"**(a) THE STATEMENT.** \u2014 No clinician's employment, contract, or privileges may be terminated, non-renewed, or materially restricted except on a prior written statement of the **specific facts** relied on; a statement reciting a policy or conclusion without the underlying conduct does not satisfy this subsection.\n\n**(b) NOTICE AND RESPONSE.** \u2014 Not less than the schedule period's notice, and an opportunity to respond on the record \u2014 except where the stated facts are an imminent threat to patient safety, in which case restriction may be immediate and the statement and response follow within the schedule period.\n\n**(c) ONLY THE STATED FACTS.** \u2014 In any proceeding, facts not stated may not be relied on.\n\n**(d) INEFFECTIVE WITHOUT THE STATEMENT.** \u2014 A termination or restriction not satisfying this section is ineffective, and compensation and privileges continue until compliance.\n\n**(e)** Nothing in this section requires cause; it requires the stated-facts procedure, and \u00a7 28-1 states the only forbidden grounds."},{"anchor":"sec-28-4","heading":"\u00a7 28-4. Remedies","part":null,"body":"A clinician aggrieved may bring a civil action: reinstatement of position, contract, or privileges; back pay and lost compensation; damages equal to the back pay, in addition; fees, expert fees, and costs; the \u00a7 62(e) conforming applies; and NPDB reports arising from an action found violative shall be voided and corrected. **No arbitration agreement bars the action, and \u00a7 46-6(b)'s concurrent jurisdiction applies.**"},{"anchor":"sec-28-5","heading":"\u00a7 28-5. Closure","part":null,"body":"No agreement waives this title; a term purporting to is void; peer-review confidentiality is not a shield against \u00a7 28-1 liability and not pierced beyond what the action requires."},{"anchor":"sec-29-1","heading":"\u00a7 29-1. Boarding, measured","part":null,"body":"**(a)** **Boarding hours** are the hours an individual who is ultimately admitted from a dedicated emergency department remains in that department beyond the grace window set in the schedule, measured **from the timestamps of arrival at and physical departure from the department** \u2014 never from the timing of any order, decision, or classification.\n\n**(b)** Timestamps are the facility's own records; a record contradicted by the facility's contemporaneous systems is a false statement on the model of section 14-5(b) of the Ending Surprise Ambulance Bills and Fake ERs Act."},{"anchor":"sec-29-2","heading":"\u00a7 29-2. The payment consequence \u2014 proportional, per hour, with the exits stated","part":null,"body":"**(a)** For a stay beginning with emergency-department presentation, the payment for the stay is reduced by the schedule percentage **for each boarding hour** \u2014 continuous, uncapped by any step, and computed automatically from the \u00a7 29-1 timestamps.\n\n**(b) THE EXITS, AND THEY ARE ALL OF THEM.** \u2014 Hours are excluded from the computation \u2014\n\n**(1)** from the facility's **timely request to the Network** for transfer of the individual (recorded under section 14-2(f-4) of the Ending Surprise Ambulance Bills and Fake ERs Act) until the Network identifies an accepting facility;\n\n**(2)** after acceptance, **while transport is pending**; and\n\n**(3)** during a **declared emergency or mass-casualty event** affecting the facility, for its duration.\n\n**No other exclusion exists, and the omission of one is a determination of Congress and not a gap.**\n\n**(c) THE PATIENT IS HELD HARMLESS, TWICE.** \u2014 No reduction under this section affects any individual's cost sharing, coverage, or liability; and no facility may alter an individual's care, admission, or disposition **to manage the computation** \u2014 an adverse action taken by reason of this section's arithmetic is a violation of \u00a7 28's machinery."},{"anchor":"sec-29-3","heading":"\u00a7 29-3. The decompression protocol \u2014 have it, publish it, follow it","part":null,"body":"**(a)** As a condition of the payments under section 6 of the Keeping Hospitals Open Act, a hospital shall maintain and publish a **full-capacity protocol**: the occupancy threshold at which it activates, the actions taken on activation \u2014 including moving admitted patients to inpatient hallway or surge placement, surgical-schedule smoothing, and discharge acceleration \u2014 and the officer responsible.\n\n**(b)** Compliance is scored **proportionally** under the \u00a7 6-13 machinery \u2014 the protocol followed in part is credited in part; no all-or-nothing element may be constructed.\n\n**(c) PSYCHIATRIC BOARDING IS NAMED.** \u2014 The protocol shall address psychiatric boarding specifically, and every measure and publication under this title states psychiatric boarding separately."},{"anchor":"sec-29-4","heading":"\u00a7 29-4. Publication, both sides","part":null,"body":"**(a)** Boarding-hour distributions, by facility and month, including psychiatric boarding separately, are published through the \u00a7 16 machinery as a ministerial duty \u2014 the statutory measure aligned with, and satisfied by, the ECAT reporting the findings describe.\n\n**(b) THE RECEIVING SIDE IS VISIBLE TOO.** \u2014 Where the Network identifies a facility as having current reported capacity and capability for a boarding transfer and that facility declines, the decline \u2014 recorded by name and role under section 14-7(b) of the Ending Surprise Ambulance Bills and Fake ERs Act \u2014 is **published in aggregate by facility and month.**"},{"anchor":"sec-29-5","heading":"\u00a7 29-5. Relation","part":null,"body":"Nothing in this title compels any facility to accept any transfer, modifies section 1867 of the Social Security Act or the Ending Surprise Ambulance Bills and Fake ERs Act, or authorizes any allocation authority."},{"anchor":"sec-30-1","heading":"\u00a7 30-1. The ratios, and the schedule that reaches them","part":null,"body":"**(a)** A hospital receiving payment under section 6 of the Keeping Hospitals Open Act or the program shall staff each unit at no fewer registered nurses per patient than the ratio in effect for that unit type under the phase schedule.\n\n**(b) THE PHASE SCHEDULE.** \u2014 The schedule states, for each unit type, the terminal ratio and the annual steps from enactment to the terminal year \u2014 **published in the statute, tightening on dates certain, with no step contingent on any finding.**\n\n**(c) AT ALL TIMES.** \u2014 Ratios apply at all times, including meal and break periods, met by relief coverage.\n\n**(d) NO EXEMPTION CLASSES.** \u2014 No waiver, exemption, or rural carve-out exists under this section; the omission is a determination of Congress and not a gap."},{"anchor":"sec-30-2","heading":"\u00a7 30-2. The high-water rule during the phase","part":null,"body":"No facility may reduce unit staffing below its level as of the date of introduction of this Act; a facility already at or above a scheduled step holds its level."},{"anchor":"sec-30-3","heading":"\u00a7 30-3. The pipeline, in the same title","part":null,"body":"**(a)** There are appropriated, permanently: funding for nursing faculty positions, clinical training slots, and nurse-education capacity, at the levels set in the schedule \u2014 **sized to the supply the phase schedule's terminal ratios require**.\n\n**(b)** Distribution follows measured shortage, on the section 12-5 of the Keeping Hospitals Open Act allocation form \u2014 never existing footprint."},{"anchor":"sec-30-4","heading":"\u00a7 30-4. Measurement and consequence","part":null,"body":"**(a)** Unit staffing is reported from scheduling and payroll records (the data facilities already hold), published quarterly by facility; a report contradicted by the facility's contemporaneous records is a false statement on the section 14-5(b) of the Ending Surprise Ambulance Bills and Fake ERs Act model.\n\n**(b)** Shortfalls draw the \u00a7 6-13 proportional machinery \u2014 credited in part for compliance in part, never all-or-nothing \u2014 and persistent shortfall routes to the section 6-10(e) of the Keeping Hospitals Open Act graduated ladder. **A shortfall during the phase attributable to a documented, diligent, unfilled posting is scored against the pipeline's adequacy in the \u00a7 30-5 report, not against the facility**."},{"anchor":"sec-30-5","heading":"\u00a7 30-5. The monitor with a corrector","part":null,"body":"The Center reports annually: staffing levels against the schedule, wage trends, agency-staffing utilization, pipeline output, and unfilled-posting volumes \u2014 and where supply is failing the schedule, **the report states the pipeline amendment that would correct it.**"},{"anchor":"sec-30-6","heading":"\u00a7 30-6. Workplace violence prevention","part":null,"body":"**(a)** Each facility shall maintain, publish, and follow a workplace-violence prevention program: risk assessment, incident tracking through the \u00a7 16 reporting machinery, training, and post-incident response.\n\n**(b)** Scored proportionally under the same machinery; incident data published in aggregate."},{"anchor":"sec-31-1","heading":"\u00a7 31-1. National registration","part":null,"body":"**(a)** A health-care provider holding an unrestricted license in the provider's home State may register nationally. Registration is **ministerial** \u2014 made through the national provider identifier infrastructure, effective on filing, confirmed by record; no application is adjudicated, no board is created, and no discretion exists.\n\n**(b)** A nationally registered provider may practice the licensed profession, within its home-State scope, **in every State and territory** \u2014 in person and by telehealth \u2014 and a State law prohibiting or conditioning that practice, other than as this title permits, is preempted.\n\n**(c) DUAL TRACK, STATED.** \u2014 State licensure continues unchanged; a provider may hold either or both; nothing requires any provider to register or any State to alter its own licensure."},{"anchor":"sec-31-2","heading":"\u00a7 31-2. What a State keeps","part":null,"body":"**(a) THE FEE, AT PARITY.** \u2014 A State may require a nationally registered provider practicing within it to file a notice and pay an annual fee **not exceeding the fee the State charges its own licensees of the same profession**.\n\n**(b) CONDUCT JURISDICTION.** \u2014 Practice within a State is subject to that State's generally applicable standards of care, facility rules, and prescription laws; and a State may, for cause arising from practice within it, **restrict the provider's local privilege** after notice and opportunity to respond \u2014 reporting the restriction to the home State and the data bank. **The NLC remote-State form; the \u00a7 28 due-process machinery applies to facility-side action.**\n\n**(c)** A State restriction of the local privilege affects practice in that State only; **suspension or revocation of the license itself belongs to the home State alone.**"},{"anchor":"sec-31-3","heading":"\u00a7 31-3. The home State","part":null,"body":"**(a)** A provider's **home State** is the State in which the provider practiced the most hours in the preceding year, **computed from claims place-of-service records held by the program** \u2014 measured, never merely attested \u2014 with the State of residence as the default where records are insufficient and for a provider entering practice.\n\n**(b)** The home State owns licensure, renewal, continuing education, and discipline; its determinations propagate through the national practitioner data bank, which every State and facility queries on the existing mandatory terms.\n\n**(c)** A change of home State follows the hours, effective at the annual computation, with the license transferring by endorsement on the receiving State's ministerial acceptance \u2014 **and no gap in licensure arises from the transfer itself.**"},{"anchor":"sec-31-4","heading":"\u00a7 31-4. Closure","part":null,"body":"The routes stated \u2014 home-State license, national registration, or both \u2014 are the routes; no Federal license, board, or examination is created; the omission of any further Federal role is a determination of Congress and not a gap. This title does not alter scope of practice, which remains the home State's law \u2014 **these bills take no side in disputes over scope of practice, and this sentence says so in text.**"},{"anchor":"sec-46-2","heading":"\u00a7 46-2. Discretionary clauses are void","part":null,"body":"**(a)** A term of a group health plan, a disability income plan, or health or disability insurance coverage that grants the plan, its administrator, its insurer, or any delegate discretionary authority to determine eligibility for benefits or to construe the plan's terms is **void**, and no such term may be given any effect \u2014 **regardless of the form, title, or characterization of the term.**\n\n**(b)** Judicial and external review of a benefit determination is **de novo**, and no deference attaches to the determination under any doctrine. **The standard of review is a rule of this statute, and the omission of a deferential standard is a determination of Congress and not a gap.**"},{"anchor":"sec-46-3","heading":"\u00a7 46-3. The duty and the liability","part":null,"body":"**(a) THE DUTY.** \u2014 A plan and any person acting for it in deciding claims shall exercise **ordinary care** in making benefit determinations under a group health plan or disability income coverage.\n\n**(b) MAKE-WHOLE LIABILITY.** \u2014 A plan that fails to exercise ordinary care in denying or delaying a benefit is liable to the participant or beneficiary for the benefit, interest from the date the benefit was due, and **the economic and noneconomic damages proximately caused by the denial or delay, including for personal injury or death.**\n\n**(c) NO PUNITIVE DAMAGES; NO CAPS.** \u2014 No punitive or exemplary damages are available under this section, **and no limitation on the amount of compensatory damages applies.** Both omissions are determinations of Congress and not gaps.\n\n**(d) WHO IS LIABLE.** \u2014 Liability runs to the plan and, where an insurer or claims administrator made or directed the determination, to that person jointly \u2014 **the party that decided pays, and an administrative-services label does not launder the decision.**"},{"anchor":"sec-46-4","heading":"\u00a7 46-4. Deadlines, and what a blown deadline is","part":null,"body":"**(a)** A claim, an internal appeal, and an external review are each decided within the period stated in the schedule, with shorter periods for urgent care on \u00a7 7-3(b)'s pattern. **Every determination states, on its face, the date the claim was received, the period that governed, and the date decided.**\n\n**(b) DEEMED APPROVAL.** \u2014 A claim or appeal not decided within its period is **approved as submitted by operation of law.** The approval may be revisited afterward only on a showing that the claim was fraudulent, established after notice and opportunity to respond; **no other ground reopens it, and the omission of a ground is a determination of Congress and not a gap.**"},{"anchor":"sec-46-5","heading":"\u00a7 46-5. External review first, and binding","part":null,"body":"**(a)** Before suit under \u00a7 46-3, a participant shall have obtained an external review determination under the machinery of section 2719 of the Public Health Service Act, as strengthened by this subsection: the reviewer is independent on \u00a7 19-4(e)'s conflict-free terms, the determination binds the plan, and the review runs on subsection \u00a7 46-4(a)'s clock.\n\n**(b)** Suit lies **without** external review where the plan blew any deadline, where the claim was deemed approved and not honored, or where the injury has already occurred."},{"anchor":"sec-46-6","heading":"\u00a7 46-6. Fees, forum, and tax character","part":null,"body":"**(a)** A prevailing participant or beneficiary recovers reasonable attorney's fees and costs; a plan recovers fees only on a finding that the action was frivolous.\n\n**(b)** State and federal courts have concurrent jurisdiction under this title.\n\n**(c)** Section 62(e) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 is amended to include an action under this title, so fee awards are not taxed as the participant's income before they reach counsel."},{"anchor":"sec-46-7","heading":"\u00a7 46-7. Scope, and what is preserved","part":null,"body":"**(a)** This title applies to group health plans and disability income coverage, **whether insured or self-insured.**\n\n**(b)** Nothing in this title affects pension or retirement plans, alters section 514 of ERISA except that the remedy of this title exists, or displaces any State remedy applicable to insured coverage under the saving clause \u2014 **a State bad-faith remedy and this title's remedy coexist, and the participant elects.**\n\n**(c)** For coverage under the program and its chassis plans, the program's own remedy machinery governs."},{"anchor":"sec-47-1","heading":"\u00a7 47-1. One rulebook \u2014 definitions and the rule of one enactment","part":null,"body":"**(a) COVERED COVERAGE.** \u2014 In this title and in every rule it consolidates, \"covered coverage\" means: a group health plan, whether or not insured; health insurance coverage offered in the group or individual market; and a plan offered under the Public Option Act. **Disability income coverage is within the term where a rule says so.**\n\n**(b) THE RULE OF ONE ENACTMENT.** \u2014 A requirement of this title, and each rule consolidated by \u00a7 47-2, is enacted **once**, applies to all covered coverage of its stated scope, and has the effect it would have if separately enacted in title XXVII of the Public Health Service Act, in part 7 of subtitle B of title I of the Employee Retirement Income Security Act of 1974, and in chapter 100 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986. **No requirement of this title fails to reach any covered coverage by reason of the statute in which a predecessor rule sat.**\n\n**(c) AND PROSPECTIVELY.** \u2014 A future federal health-coverage requirement stated for covered coverage applies per its terms notwithstanding placement, unless it expressly narrows its scope."},{"anchor":"sec-47-2","heading":"\u00a7 47-2. The consolidation","part":null,"body":"**(a)** Each of the following rules is re-enacted as a single rule of this title, on its strongest existing terms, applicable to all covered coverage \u2014 **(1)** the ban on preexisting-condition exclusions; **(2)** the ban on lifetime and annual dollar limits; **(3)** the ban on rescission; **(4)** preventive services without cost sharing; **(5)** dependent coverage to age 26; **(6)** nondiscrimination based on health status; **(7)** internal appeals and external review, as strengthened by \u00a7 46; and **(8)** the patient protections respecting emergency services and choice of provider \u2014 and the predecessor provisions in the three codes are **succeeded** \u2014 each continues as the single rule, every cross-reference to a predecessor resolves to it, and no substantive term changes except as a row states.\n\n**(b)** Rulemaking authority, where a consolidated rule carries any, is exercised jointly as under current law, and **a rule issued for the consolidated rule is effective for all covered coverage at once** \u2014 the tri-agency machinery may continue; the triplicate *effect* may not."},{"anchor":"sec-47-3","heading":"\u00a7 47-3. The harmonized scopes \u2014 the UP moves","part":null,"body":"Each of the following existing rules is extended to all covered coverage:\n\n**(a)** the network directory-accuracy and reliance rules of \u00a7 7-1, and the network-composition parity measures of \u00a7 7-2, **including to self-insured group health plans**;\n\n**(b)** the prior-authorization rules of \u00a7 7-3 \u2014 clinician review, clocks, deemed authorization, gold-carding \u2014 **to every covered coverage**;\n\n**(c)** the discretionary-clause voiding and de novo review of \u00a7 46-2, and the claims machinery of \u00a7\u00a7 46-4 and 46-5 \u2014 **one claims-and-review procedure for all covered coverage**, in place of the separate ERISA-procedure and ACA-procedure tracks;\n\n**(d)** the mental health and substance use disorder parity requirements, administered as one rule with \u00a7 7-2's measured standard; and\n\n**(e)** the transparency rules \u2014 machine-readable rates and advance explanations of benefits \u2014 on the broadest existing scope."},{"anchor":"sec-47-4","heading":"\u00a7 47-4. The stated exceptions \u2014 the status-quo test, applied and recorded","part":null,"body":"The following are **not** harmonized, each deliberately, and the omission of each is a determination of Congress and not a gap:\n\n**(a) RATING.** \u2014 No community-rating or adjusted-community-rating requirement extends to large-group or self-insured coverage.\n\n**(b) BENEFIT DESIGN.** \u2014 No essential-health-benefits package mandate extends to large-group or self-insured coverage beyond the benefit classes current law already reaches.\n\n**(c) RETENTION/MLR.** \u2014 The medical-loss-ratio machinery keeps its insured-market scope; a self-insured plan has no premium and the \u00a7 9-2 retention machinery is not extended to it."},{"anchor":"sec-47-5","heading":"\u00a7 47-5. Grandfathered plans \u2014 the status retires","part":null,"body":"**(a)** Grandfathered health plan status under section 1251 of the Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act terminates on the schedule date, and every covered coverage complies with the consolidated rules from that date.\n\n**(b)** The schedule date is not earlier than the second plan year after enactment, and **nothing terminates any person's coverage** \u2014 the plan continues; the exemptions end."},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE","part":null,"body":"*(Reserved.)*\n\n**\u00a7 27 values:** 1. Transition ratchet period (\u00a7 27-4(b)): [___] years (order of a decade)\n2. Purchase-lane statement contents cap (\u00a7 27-5)\n\n**\u00a7 28 values:** 1. Notice period (\u00a7 28-3(b)) \u00b7 2. Post-restriction statement/response window (imminent-threat path)\n\n**\u00a7 29 values:** 1. Grace window (\u00a7 29-1(a)) **sized generously for legitimate workup** \u00b7 2. Per-hour reduction percentage (\u00a7 29-2(a)) \u00b7 3. Timely-request window (\u00a7 29-2(b)(1))\n\n**\u00a7 30 values:** 1. Terminal ratios by unit type (California values as working assumption) \u00b7 2. Phase steps and terminal year \u00b7 3. Pipeline appropriations levels \u00b7 4. Prevention-program elements floor\n\n**\u00a7 31 values:** 1. Annual hours-computation date (\u00a7 31-3) \u00b7 2. Notice-filing contents cap (\u00a7 31-2(a), the one-page class)\n\n**\u00a7 46 values:** 1. \u00a7 46-4(a) decision periods (claim / appeal / external review / urgent) \u00b7 2. Effective date \u2014 applies to determinations made after it (no retroactive liability)\n\n**\u00a7 47 values:** 1. \u00a7 47-5 grandfather termination date \u00b7 2. \u00a7 47-3 extension effective dates (plan-year boundaries, dated steps)"},{"anchor":"sec-0-6","heading":"\u00a7 0-6. References to sections of related Acts","part":null,"body":"**(a) SCOPE.** \u2014 A reference in this Act to a section not contained in this Act is a reference to that section of the One Medicare Act, the Medicare Covers Teeth, Eyes, and Ears Act, the Long-Term Care Act, the Public Option Act, the Lowest Drug Price Wins Act, the Keeping Hospitals Open Act, the Patient Safety and Compensation Act, the Ending Surprise Ambulance Bills and Fake ERs Act, and the Real Employer Act, as the context requires.\n\n**(b) IF THE RELATED ACT IS NOT IN EFFECT.** \u2014 Where a section referred to under subsection (a) is not in effect, the reference applies as that section read on the date of enactment of this Act.\n\n**(c) IF THE SECTION HAS NEVER TAKEN EFFECT.** \u2014 Where a provision of this Act conditions a consequence on a report, program, payment, or determination established by a section not contained in this Act, and that section has never taken effect, **the condition is inapplicable and the remainder of the provision operates according to its terms.**\n\n**(d) THE PROGRAM.** \u2014 A reference in this Act to \"the program\" is a reference to coverage and benefits under the One Medicare Act."},{"anchor":"sec-0-7","heading":"\u00a7 0-7. Severability","part":null,"body":"**(a)** If any provision of this Act, or the application of any provision to any person or circumstance, is held invalid, the remainder of this Act and the application of the provision to any other person or circumstance are not affected.\n\n**(b) NOT ONE INTEGRATED SCHEME.** \u2014 The provisions of this Act are separate and independent. **A court shall not treat this Act as a single integrated scheme, and shall not infer from the invalidity of one provision that the Congress would not have enacted the remainder.**"},{"anchor":"sec-0-8","heading":"\u00a7 0-8. Effective dates","part":null,"body":"**(a) IN GENERAL.** \u2014 Except as otherwise provided, this Act takes effect on the first day of the first plan year beginning after the date that is one year after the date of enactment.\n\n**(b) SCHEDULE VALUES.** \u2014 Where a provision refers to an amount, period, threshold, or rate set in the schedule, that value is the value stated in the SCHEDULE to this Act."}],"asOf":"2026-11-18"}}}};
